{"builder":"Make one pull request that meets every acceptance line of issue https://github.com/o/r/issues/23:\n- math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\n- clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\n- README.md documents every function math.js exports\n- npm test passes\n\nCurrent state: math.js is CommonJS and exports { add, subtract, multiply } via module.exports. README.md has an \"## API\" section with a \"### name(args)\" heading plus a short description and js example per function, but it documents only add and multiply. subtract is missing. Tests live in test/math.test.js and run with `npm test` (`node --test`).\n\nSteps:\n1. In math.js, add a function clamp(x, lo, hi) with a short doc comment, matching the style of the existing functions. If lo > hi, throw new RangeError(...) with a clear message. Otherwise return lo when x < lo, hi when x > hi, and x itself when it is in range (lo and hi count as inside the range). Add clamp to module.exports and keep add, subtract and multiply exported.\n2. In README.md's \"## API\" section, add a \"### subtract(a, b)\" entry and a \"### clamp(x, lo, hi)\" entry in the same format as the existing ones. Each needs a description and a js example. The clamp entry must say that a RangeError is thrown when lo is greater than hi. Every name in math.js's module.exports must end up with its own heading in the API section.\n3. In test/math.test.js, import clamp and add tests for: a value below the range (e.g. clamp(-5, 0, 100) === 0), a value above it (clamp(140, 0, 100) === 100), a value inside it (clamp(42, 0, 100) === 42), the boundaries lo and hi, lo === hi, and assert.throws(() => clamp(5, 10, 0), RangeError).\n4. Run `npm test` and make sure every test passes, with no failures and no skips added. Then open the pull request.","verifier":"Check the pull request head against the four acceptance lines of issue https://github.com/o/r/issues/23. Make one check per line, 